{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>I am HaShem, the Elohim of Abraham your father and the Elohim of Isaac.</b> We find concerning Moses our master, peace be upon him, that close to his death he was called 'a man of Elohim' (Deuteronomy 33:1), but after his actual death the distinctive Name was invoked upon him, as it says, ״וַיָּמָת מֹשֶׁה עֶבֶד ה׳״ (Deuteronomy 34:5). So too Abraham, who had already died — the distinctive Name was invoked upon him, and He said, 'the HaShem, the Elohim of Abraham'; but Isaac, who was only reckoned as though dead because he was blind and confined to the house, and in any case was not actually dead — therefore only the name Elohim was invoked upon him, for this name is shared between HaShem and created beings, as it is written, ״רְאֵה נְתַתִּיךָ אֱלֹהִים לְפַרְעֹה״ (Exodus 7:1). And as for why He mentioned 'your father' in connection with Abraham but not in connection with Isaac — either to remove from the hearts of those who say 'Sarah conceived from Abimelech,' or to teach that the children of children are regarded as one's own children. And as for what is written, 'the land upon which you lie' — He folded the whole land beneath him. See the explanation of this matter in parashat Genesis, on the verse ״אֲשֶׁר לֻקַּח מִשָּׁם״ (Genesis 3:23) — for there is the Even Shetiyah, and there is the center of the world, and one who lies in that place is as though he lies upon the whole earth, for from there all the climes spread forth. Therefore it says, 'and you shall spread abroad to the west' — that is, from this place you shall break forth to all four directions, for through the acquisition of this place you shall acquire all four directions of the world, for from there is their source."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:48.692836416Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}